---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import CTA from "../components/CTA.astro";
import { breadcrumbs } from "../lib/schema";
---
<Base
  title="Batch COAs — Third-Party Lab Certificates on File"
  description="Every batch on this desk carries a third-party certificate of analysis with the lab name and batch ID. Ask for the COA on the exact batch you are quoted."
  path="/coa/"
  schema={[breadcrumbs([{ name: "Home", url: "/" }, { name: "COA", url: "/coa/" }])]}
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Third-party · Per batch · Matching IDs</p>
  <h1>Batch COAs</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Every vial leaves here with a batch number, and every batch has a certificate of analysis from an
    independent lab. No batch, no sale. The COA is a PDF, it carries the lab's own reference, and the batch ID
    on it matches the vial in your hand.
  </p>

  <h2>What is on a COA here</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Lab name and its own report reference.</li>
    <li>The batch ID, identical to the one printed on the vial.</li>
    <li>Identity confirmation and purity figure by HPLC.</li>
    <li>Mass-spec confirmation where the lab runs it.</li>
    <li>Fill weight and appearance.</li>
    <li>Date of analysis.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to get yours</h2>
  <p>
    Ask in the chat before you pay. You get the COA for the exact batch being quoted, not a stock sample. If the
    batch changes between quote and dispatch, you get the new COA before dispatch, unprompted.
  </p>

  <h2>How to read one</h2>
  <p>
    Purity is an HPLC percentage and anything under 98 is worth a question. Identity should be a confirmed match,
    not "consistent with". A COA with no batch ID is decoration. A COA with a batch ID that does not match your
    vial is worse than none, because it means the paperwork and the product came from different places.
  </p>

  <h2>Why this is the whole argument</h2>
  <p>
    Peptides are cheap to fake and expensive to test. The COA is the only part of the transaction you can check
    independently of the seller's word. It is also the reason a Telegram desk beats a webshop here: on a webshop
    the COA is a stock image on a product page, if it exists at all. Here it is a specific document for a
    specific batch, sent before payment.
  </p>

  <CTA
    src="/coa/"
    label="COA page"
    heading="Ask for a batch COA now"
    body="Name the peptide. You get the batch ID and its COA before you are asked for anything."
  />
</Base>